<%# The error summary for a form, in one place.

    There were three shapes for this: `rounded-2xl border-red-200 bg-red-50` with a bolded "Error:" on
    the student pages, `bg-red-50 px-3 py-2 rounded-lg` with an `<h2>` on the classroom form, and
    Devise's own partial. Three definitions of one thing is the drift mechanism this codebase keeps
    rediscovering.

    design.md's alert shape: a red-50 tint, a red-200 hairline, red-700 ink, at the panel radius.
    Measured: red-700 on red-50 is 6.48:1.

    **A heading that counts, not a bare "Error:".** "2 errors prohibited this student from being saved"
    tells the reader how many things to look for; "Error:" over a list of two does not. That is GOV.UK's
    error summary, Polaris's and Primer's.

    **No dismiss.** It describes the form as it stands and is rebuilt on every submit, so hiding it
    hides the list of what is still wrong. `role="alert"` so it is announced when it appears, and
    `tabindex="-1"` with an id so a caller can move focus to it.

    Locals:
      record - the model whose errors these are
      subject - optional noun for the sentence; defaults to the model name %>
<% return unless record.errors.any? %>

<% subject = local_assigns.fetch(:subject, record.class.model_name.human.downcase) %>

<div id="error_explanation"
     class="rounded-2xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50 px-4 py-3 text-red-700"
     role="alert"
     tabindex="-1"
     data-testid="form-errors">
  <p class="text-sm font-semibold">
    <%= pluralize(record.errors.count, "error") %> stopped this <%= subject %> being saved
  </p>

  <ul class="mt-1 list-disc space-y-0.5 pl-5 text-sm">
    <% record.errors.each do |error| %>
      <li><%= error.full_message %></li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
</div>
